{"availability":"available","full":"elixir-localize/localize","name":"localize","description":"Localization for Elixir","category":"Other","language":"Elixir","stars":67,"forks":11,"created":"2026-01-11T00:32:14Z","updated":"2026-10-04T07:49:56Z","license":null,"branch":"main","homepage":null,"topics":[],"demo":"https://playground.elixir-localize.com/","color":"#25392f","ink":"#d1e5c5","lastCheckedAt":"2026-10-04T08:15:58.621Z","lastAttemptAt":"2026-10-04T08:15:58.621Z","lastAvailableAt":"2026-10-04T08:15:58.621Z","metrics":[{"at":"2026-10-04T08:15:58.621Z","stars":67,"forks":11}],"technologies":[],"overview":{"kind":"readme","version":1,"paragraphs":["Locale-aware formatting, validation, and data access for Elixir, built on the Unicode CLDR repository.","Localize consolidates the functionality of the excldr library family into a single package. No compile-time backend modules or code generation is required — all CLDR data is loaded at runtime and cached in :persistentterm.","Localize 1.4 is built on CLDR 49; Localize.version/0 reports the CLDR release a given version ships."],"features":["Numbers — format integers, decimals, percentages, and currencies with locale-appropriate grouping, decimal separators, and symbols.","Number parsing — parse locale-formatted strings back into numbers, including scanning numbers out of arbitrary text.","RBNF — rule-based number formatting: spell-out (\"one hundred twenty-three\"), ordinals (\"42nd\"), Roman numerals, and other algorithmic systems.","Plural rules — classify numbers into CLDR cardinal and ordinal plural categories for grammatically correct pluralization.","Minimal pairs — CLDR's worked examples of a locale's plural, ordinal, case and gender forms, useful for showing a sample or checking that a rule selects the form it should."],"sourceUrl":"https://github.com/elixir-localize/localize/blob/main/README.md","fingerprint":"b496f5004b29797f74e0c6d5c58066cc1c411eaf6fec9880ac1e990a01e8476f","extractedAt":"2026-10-04T06:12:0